## Vendor portability (write once, target many AI tools)

Most teams pair Claude Code with at least one other tool: Cursor,
Cline, Codex, Aider, Copilot, Windsurf. The rules should apply to
all of them without manual duplication.

### Canonical source: `AGENTS.md`

`AGENTS.md` is the open standard at <https://agentskills.io>. Other
vendors' config files import or symlink to it.

```
project-root/
├── AGENTS.md                    canonical instructions
├── CLAUDE.md                    `@AGENTS.md` + Claude-Code specifics
├── .cursorrules                 (symlink to AGENTS.md, or imports)
├── .clinerules                  (symlink or imports)
├── .windsurfrules               (symlink or imports)
└── .github/
    └── copilot-instructions.md  (symlink or imports)
```

### Per-vendor config locations

| Vendor | Project config | User-level | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | `CLAUDE.md` or `.claude/CLAUDE.md` | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` | Cited: <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory> |
| Claude.ai (web/desktop) | manual paste | manual paste | No native file format |
| OpenAI Codex CLI | `AGENTS.md` | `~/.codex/instructions/` | Canonical home for AGENTS.md |
| Cursor | `.cursorrules` | `~/.cursor/rules` | Project file wins |
| Cline | `.clinerules` | settings UI | VS Code extension |
| Aider | `AGENTS.md` + `.aider.conf.yml` | `~/.aider.conf.yml` | Reads `AGENTS.md` natively |
| Windsurf | `.windsurfrules` | settings UI | |
| GitHub Copilot | `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | settings UI | Repo-scoped |

### Pattern 0: let `ai-configurator` do it

If `ai-configurator` is installed, skip the manual patterns. One
command writes `AGENTS.md` + every per-vendor file the project's
vendor list selects:

```bash
# Compose the canonical AGENTS.md from CLAUDE.md + decision-pack fragments
ai-configurator compose-agents-md

# Drop AGENTS.md + .cursorrules + .windsurfrules + .clinerules + ...
# into <project> based on the configured vendors
ai-configurator project-install <project> \
    --vendor=aider --vendor=cursor --vendor=windsurf \
    --vendor=copilot --vendor=codex --vendor=cline
```

`project-install` skips existing files unless `--force`. It also
auto-composes `AGENTS.md` if it isn't on disk yet. Without `--vendor`
flags, it falls back to the `vendors` field of your loaded config.

The manual patterns below stay valid for users who don't have the
configurator on this machine.

### Pattern A: `@AGENTS.md` import (preferred for Claude Code)

```markdown
# CLAUDE.md
@AGENTS.md

## Claude Code-specific
Use `/audit-prose` when reviewing PR descriptions.
```

Claude Code's `@path` import inlines `AGENTS.md` at session start.
See <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#import-additional-files>.

### Pattern B: symlinks (works for tools that don't support imports)

```bash
ln -s AGENTS.md .cursorrules
ln -s AGENTS.md .clinerules
ln -s AGENTS.md .windsurfrules
mkdir -p .github && ln -s ../AGENTS.md .github/copilot-instructions.md
```

On Windows, `ln -s` needs Administrator or Developer Mode. The
alternative is a copy + a pre-commit hook to keep them in sync.

### Pattern C: per-vendor extension files

When a vendor needs guidance the others don't:

```markdown
# CLAUDE.md
@AGENTS.md

## Claude Code only
- Use the `/audit-prose` skill before approving PR descriptions.
- The `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/` auto-memory system is
  authoritative for cross-session state.

# .cursorrules
@AGENTS.md

## Cursor only
- @ symbol completions reference files; prefer them over pasting paths.
```

### Audit

Run `/audit-vendor-config` after adding a new vendor file to check
for drift. The audit reports rules in CLAUDE.md that aren't in
AGENTS.md (and vice versa) so the canonical source stays canonical.

### Don't

- **Don't write rules in CLAUDE.md alone** when the same rule
  applies in Cursor + Codex + Aider. Hoist to AGENTS.md.
- **Don't keep .cursorrules out of git** unless it has personal
  preferences. Team-shared rules belong in the canonical file.
- **Don't paste the same content into multiple files** by hand.
  Use the import/symlink pattern or a pre-commit hook.
